{
 "schema": 4,
 "question": "B. Is this measure already running in Kymenlaakso wellbeing services county (or in the HUS university hospital area that treats its patients)?\nA. Does recent evidence support the size of effect we assume: does this measure raise the share of lung cancer patients who get curative-intent treatment, and by how much?\nMeasure: Video MDT review of every stage I-III patient with the university hospital. Every patient whose cancer has not spread (stage I-III) is discussed by video with the university hospital lung team (surgeon, radiotherapy doctor, oncologist) before anyone decides against treatment that aims to cure. Today a county hospital can decide alone.",
 "area_id": "10",
 "verdict": "mixed",
 "effect_check": "weaker",
 "effect_summary_en": "One UK before-after study at one district hospital (Southend, 62 patients presented in a year) reported that the annual resection rate rose by 30% after video MDT meetings with a cardiothoracic centre began; it reports no change in the share of patients treated with curative intent.",
 "supported_en": "A 2004 UK before-after study in the Journal of Telemedicine and Telecare found that the annual resection rate rose by 30% after video MDT meetings began.",
 "not_supported_en": "The pages read do not show the assumed gain of 1.5 percentage points in the curative-intent share, and they show nothing from Finland.",
 "in_use_here": "partly",
